---
import DocsPage from "../../layouts/DocsPage.astro"
import { runGuardRows, spendRows } from "../../data/docs-content"
import { release113 } from "../../data/vector-releases"

export const prerender = true
---

<DocsPage id="tokenomics">

          <p>
            Vector prices each chat from the tokens the provider reported on the assistant messages — input,
            output, reasoning, cache reads and cache writes — at the model catalog's list price. Automations and
            parallel agents are also written to a local ledger, and an automation is refused a start once your daily
            cap is spent.
          </p>
          <dl class="doc-rows">
            {spendRows.map(([term, detail]) => (<div><dt>{term}</dt><dd>{detail}</dd></div>))}
          </dl>
          <h3 id="recommendations">Model recommendations</h3>
          <p>
            Every session feeds measured token and cost evidence into model recommendations, so the ranking is built from
            what providers actually reported rather than from list prices. A recommendation is a suggestion: a model to
            pick for a kind of task. A model gets none until it has at least three recorded runs for that kind of task,
            and an unmeasured model never outranks one that reported a real cost. Runs of the coding agents you start
            from <a href="/docs/external-agents">Host locally</a> are left out: their vendors' terms bar using them to
            rank models.
          </p>

          <h3 id="learned-defaults">Learned defaults</h3>
          <p>
            {release113.since} learned defaults are separate from recommendations, with their own data and a higher bar.
            For each repository and size of work, Vector records which effort, subagent split and subagent model got the
            build agent's checks passing, and at what cost. A setting becomes the learned default only after at least 6
            checked runs of it and of the size's own default in that repository, when it passes as often for at least
            20% less, and it applies only when you leave effort on Default. Workflow insights record numbers only: size,
            effort, subagent count, model id, outcome, steps, tokens and cost. They record no prompt or file text.
          </p>
          <p>
            {release113.since} learned defaults only ever make work cheaper. Your own picks always win. Learned defaults
            only drop Thorough's extra effort, skip subagents or use a cheaper subagent model; spend caps are unchanged.
            A model or effort level you pick, one taken from a recommendation included, is your choice: it beats a
            learned default, and runs where you, the agent or config chose the effort are left out of the statistics. The
            effort never changes while the provider's prompt cache from your last request is warm, and the primary model
            is never switched.
          </p>
          <p>
            {release113.since} unknown cost stays unknown: a run with any step that was not priced, a local, plan or free
            model's included, has no cost rather than $0, and a run whose subagents were still working is not used for
            cost. Settings → Memory → Workflow insights shows each size's runs and the learned default with the reason,
            and offers Apply, Observe only and Off. Set <code>sizing.learn</code> to <code>false</code> in
            <code>vector.json</code> to keep each size's own defaults, and <code>--pure</code> runs without learned
            defaults. Resetting every size, or erasing a repository's memory or everything in Settings → Memory, also
            clears the app's model recommendations for that repository; <code>vector memory erase</code> and
            <code>vector memory workflow reset</code> cannot reach them.
          </p>
                  <h3>Usage and streaks</h3>
          <p>
            Settings → Usage reports what Vector recorded locally: lifetime tokens split into input, output, cache and
            reasoning, the cost recorded against them, chats completed, average tokens per chat, average time per run,
            the longest run and the peak day. It also keeps a daily streak with your current and longest run, seven
            days of activity, and your top five models by share of all-time token use.
          </p>
          <h3>The Run Guard, from desktop 1.99.108</h3>
          <p>
            From desktop 1.99.108, Vector holds runs to a live cap instead of checking only before they start. Vector
            checks spend after every model step and stops within one step of the cap for each agent running at the
            time. Dollars are the reported tokens at catalog
            list price unless the provider reported what the step cost, so discounts are not reflected. The npm CLI
            (1.99.99) and the CLI that <code>vectordev.ai/install</code> serves predate it, so the terminal UI and
            <code>vector run</code> with <code>--max-cost</code> and <code>--max-steps</code> get the Run Guard with the
            next CLI release, and Windows WSL servers, which run the 1.99.99 engine desktop 1.99.108 requires, get it
            once a later desktop release requires that CLI. From desktop 1.99.109, the limits are one overall set: they
            cover all your repositories together, and a repository cannot set its own.
          </p>
          <dl class="doc-rows">
            {runGuardRows.map(([term, detail]) => (<div><dt>{term}</dt><dd>{detail}</dd></div>))}
          </dl>
</DocsPage>
